---
title: Lifecycle
name: Lifecycle
description: Put signups, sign-ins and your own product events on a contact's timeline from inside your backend — with first-party plugins for Better Auth, Convex and Lunora.
category: Guides
---

A contact's **timeline** is every event about one person in one place: the opens and
clicks Postboi observed, and the things only your application knows — a signup, a plan
change, a project created. Segments filter on it, sequences trigger from it, and an
email can read it (`{event.plan}`).

Anything can write to it with one call:

```typescript
import { mail } from 'postboi'

await mail.events.track('ada@example.com', 'project_created', { plan: 'pro', seats: 3 })
```

That is the whole API, and for most events it is all you need. The plugins below exist
for the events you'd otherwise write the same fifteen lines for in every project: the
ones your **auth layer** already knows about.

## Better Auth

`postboi/better-auth` is a plugin. Add it and signups, sign-ins and profile changes
appear on the timeline — no webhook, no endpoint, no second copy of who your users are.

```typescript
import { betterAuth } from 'better-auth'
import { postboi } from 'postboi/better-auth'

export const auth = betterAuth({
	database: db,
	plugins: [postboi()],
})
```

It writes `auth.signed_up`, `auth.email_verified`, `auth.user_updated` and
`auth.signed_in`, and upserts the contact with the user's name and your own id as
`external_id` — so `mail.events.track({ external_id: user.id }, …)` works from
anywhere else in your code.

**It never fails a sign-up.** Every call is caught and reported through `on_error` (a
console line by default). A network blip at Postboi turning into a 500 on somebody's
sign-up form would be a far worse bug than a missing timeline row.

Sign-ins are one event per session, which on a busy product is a great deal of
timeline. Switch them off, or rename anything:

```typescript
postboi({
	events: { signed_in: false, signed_up: 'user.joined' },
	attributes: (user) => ({ plan: 'free' }),
	on_error: (error) => Sentry.captureException(error),
})
```

Better Auth's session hook is handed the *session*, not the user, so recording sign-ins
needs a way to resolve one — pass `user_for_session`. Without it, sign-ins are skipped
and the plugin says so once (once, not once per login).

```typescript
postboi({
	user_for_session: async (session) =>
		db.query.users.findFirst({ where: eq(users.id, session.userId) }),
})
```

## Convex

Convex **mutations cannot do network I/O** — only actions can. So the shape is always
the same: the mutation that stores the user schedules an action, and the action tracks.

```typescript
// convex/postboi.ts
import { internalAction } from './_generated/server'
import { v } from 'convex/values'
import { track, identify } from 'postboi/convex'

export const signed_up = internalAction({
	args: { id: v.string(), email: v.string(), name: v.optional(v.string()) },
	handler: async (_ctx, args) => {
		await identify(args)
		await track(args.email, 'auth.signed_up')
	},
})
```

```typescript
// convex/users.ts
export const create = mutation({
	handler: async (ctx, args) => {
		const id = await ctx.db.insert('users', args)
		await ctx.scheduler.runAfter(0, internal.postboi.signed_up, { ...args, id })
		return id
	},
})
```

Scheduling rather than awaiting isn't a workaround, it's the better shape: the mutation
commits on its own and the event follows, so a Postboi outage delays a timeline row and
never rolls back a signup.

Set `POSTBOI_TOKEN` in the Convex dashboard. If your project uses **Clerk** for auth,
user creation is a Clerk webhook rather than a mutation — connect Clerk in the Postboi
dashboard instead and the same `auth.*` events arrive with no code at all.

## Lunora

`postboi/lunora` is an opt-in package in the convention Lunora's auth, payments and
email packages already use. Register it once and `ctx.postboi` is on every action and
background job.

```typescript
import { postboi } from 'postboi/lunora'

export default defineApp({
	packages: [postboi()],
})
```

```typescript
await ctx.postboi.identify({ id: user.id, email: user.email, name: user.name })
await ctx.postboi.track(user.email, 'project_created', { plan })
await ctx.postboi.send({ to: user.email, subject: 'Welcome', body: '<p>…</p>' })
```

Lunora bundles Better Auth, so signups need nothing extra — `auth()` from the same
import *is* the Better Auth plugin above, so the events cannot differ by which door
they came through:

```typescript
import { auth } from 'postboi/lunora'

betterAuth({ plugins: [auth()] })
```

Everything runs in your backend with your token. Nothing here is hosted.

## Or connect the source directly

If the events you want come from a system Postboi already speaks to, you don't need
code at all. **Stripe, Paddle, Lemon Squeezy, Polar, Clerk, Supabase, Shopify,
WooCommerce and PostHog** connect in the dashboard, and a **generic inbound hook**
takes JSON from anything else — you send it one request and then point at the fields of
the payload it actually received.

They write the same vocabulary these plugins do — `billing.*`, `auth.*`, `commerce.*` —
so a sequence written against `billing.payment_recovered` works whichever processor the
money moved through, and moving an event from a webhook to your own code (or back)
changes nothing downstream.

## Events you can build on

| Family      | Names                                                                                                                                                                                    |
| ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `auth.*`    | `signed_up`, `signed_in`, `email_verified`, `user_updated`, `user_deleted`                                                                                                                 |
| `billing.*` | `trial_started`, `trial_ending`, `subscription_started`, `subscription_updated`, `subscription_cancelled`, `payment_failed`, `payment_recovered`, `purchase`, `refunded`                    |
| `commerce.*`| `checkout_started`, `checkout_abandoned`, `order_placed`, `order_paid`, `order_fulfilled`, `order_cancelled`, `refunded`                                                                    |

Your own names are anything snake_case, optionally namespaced by one dot
(`project_created`, `usage.limit_reached`). The `contact.*`, `email.*`, `sms.*` and
`whatsapp.*` prefixes are Postboi's own — an open is a fact the platform observed, and
an API caller claiming one would put a fiction in the timeline every segment trusts.
